{"id": "0000046e-0000-4000-8000-00000000046e", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll survey your RDS instances and Aurora clusters and check them against best practices, with a focus on backup configuration. This covers many resources, so I'll gather the details in the background and bring you a summary.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_ViVkBvq6p92UiRIsNYoZYj", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "rds-aurora-best-practices-review", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["discovering-topology"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "Review all RDS DB instances and Aurora DB clusters for best-practices compliance and backup configuration. Produce a concise, prioritized compliance report.",
  "context": "User asked: 'Review my RDS instances and Aurora clusters for best practices compliance and backup configuration'. This is a read-only inventory/audit (no mutating calls). The agent space lives in account 123456789012, region us-east-1, but workloads can live in any associated AWS account. Use list_associations/get_associations to find all associated AWS accounts, and check us-east-1 plus any other region where RDS resources are found (check commonly used regions via describe calls, e.g. us-east-1, us-east-2, us-west-2, eu-west-1; use topology/resource listing if available to find regions with RDS resources). Use read-only use_aws calls such as rds describe_db_instances, describe_db_clusters, describe_db_cluster_snapshots / describe_db_snapshots (to verify recent automated/manual snapshots exist), describe_db_parameter_groups, describe_pending_maintenance_actions, describe_db_proxies if relevant, and optionally AWS Backup (list_backup_plans, list_protected_resources) to see if instances/clusters are covered by backup plans.",
  "resources": ["All RDS DB instances", "All Aurora DB clusters (and their member instances)", "Account 123456789012 and other associated accounts"],
  "timeframe": "Current state (as of 2026-11-17T20:06:00Z); snapshot recency over the last 30 days",
  "criteria": "BACKUP: BackupRetentionPeriod (flag 0 = disabled; <7 days as weak; recommend 7-35 for prod), preferred backup window, latest restorable time, existence/recency of automated and manual snapshots, copy-tag-to-snapshot, cross-region snapshot/backup copy, AWS Backup plan coverage, Aurora backtrack (if MySQL-compatible), deletion protection, final snapshot on delete (SkipFinalSnapshot setting not directly visible; note deletion protection instead). AVAILABILITY: Multi-AZ for instances, Aurora clusters with 2+ instances across AZs, auto minor version upgrade, engine version supportability/deprecation (extended support), pending maintenance actions. SECURITY: StorageEncrypted/KMS key, PubliclyAccessible, IAM database authentication, SSL/TLS enforcement via parameter group (rds.force_ssl), default vs custom parameter groups, security group exposure (0.0.0.0/0 on DB ports), master credentials managed in Secrets Manager. OBSERVABILITY: Performance Insights, Enhanced Monitoring, log exports to CloudWatch, CloudWatch alarms for key DB metrics if easily checked. COST/OPS: previous-generation or burstable instance classes in prod-looking DBs, storage autoscaling, gp2 vs gp3, idle/stopped instances, tags. Output format: (1) inventory table with account/region, identifier, engine+version, class, Multi-AZ, encryption, backup retention, deletion protection, public access; (2) findings grouped by severity (High/Medium/Low) with resource identifiers and a recommended fix for each; (3) a short list of what is compliant. Keep the final report concise and do not dump raw API output. If no RDS/Aurora resources are found, say which accounts/regions were checked."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Audit RDS and Aurora best practices and backups"}], "created_at": null}

